Neither: JavaScript has **no built-in `Array.prototype.shuffle()`**. Calling it will normally throw a `TypeError`, unless a library or polyfill has added it.

To shuffle a copy, you can use Fisher–Yates:

```js
function shuffled(array) {
  const result = [...array];

  for (let i = result.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [result[i], result[j]] = [result[j], result[i]];
  }

  return result;
}
```

This leaves the original array unchanged. To shuffle in place, use `array` directly instead of creating `result`.
